          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse and dense retrieval methods to optimize search results across heterogeneous query types.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Single well-formed source describing production patterns; awaiting independent corroboration.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets. This dual-method approach captures both exact term matches and semantic similarity, making it particularly effective for queries mixing rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent.\\n\\n### Merging Strategies\\n\\nA common merging technique is **reciprocal rank fusion**, which combines the ranked lists from sparse and dense retrievers into a single unified ranking.\\n\\n### Status in Production\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, consistently outperforming either sparse or dense retrieval alone on real-world query distributions.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-02\\n\u0022}"
